# Target C-II — universal `8+7` complete metric basis for support seven

**Date:** 2026-09-19  
**Status:** exact structural reduction; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** every generic support-seven reciprocal branch (`H6`, `H7`, or `H8`) with no reciprocal collinear triple  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support7_complete_metric_basis_2026_09_19.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The earlier metric reductions used the fourteen present non-ground relation edges. Their graph has seven vertices and fourteen edges, hence cycle rank eight. Those eight sine-law cycle identities are necessary, but they are not the complete reciprocal metric condition.

All seven non-ground reciprocal points exist even when a support relation is missing. Consequently all twenty-one geometric pairs exist, and every missing support edge still gives an ordinary geometric chord of the reciprocal point set. The complete radial metric condition lives on `K_7`, whose cycle rank is fifteen.

This note gives an exact decomposition

\[
\boxed{15=8+7}
\]

that works for every labelled support-seven missing graph and is independent of whether the reciprocal hull type is `H6`, `H7`, or `H8`.

## 1. Sine-law cochain

For distinct non-ground vertices `i,j in {1,...,7}`, define

\[
A_{ij}=\angle 0ij,
\qquad
\rho_i=|y_i-y_0|.
\]

In triangle `0ij`, the law of sines gives

\[
\frac{\rho_j}{\rho_i}
=
\frac{\sin A_{ij}}{\sin A_{ji}}.
\]

Thus the antisymmetric cochain

\[
g_{ij}
=
\log\sin A_{ij}-\log\sin A_{ji}
\]

must equal the potential difference

\[
g_{ij}=\log\rho_j-\log\rho_i.
\]

Metric consistency is therefore exactness of `g`.

## 2. Present relation graph: eight conditions

For support seven, the missing non-ground graph is 2-regular on seven vertices. It has seven missing edges, so the present relation graph has

\[
21-7=14
\]

edges. For both missing-graph types `C7` and `C3+C4`, the present graph is connected. Choose any spanning tree `T` of the present graph. It has six edges, leaving eight present non-tree edges.

Each present non-tree edge closes one fundamental cycle with `T`. Requiring zero cochain sum on those eight cycles is equivalent to exactness of `g` on the present relation graph.

## 3. Missing chords: seven additional independent conditions

Once the eight present-cycle identities hold, the values of `g` on the spanning tree determine a unique vertex potential up to an additive constant. Every one of the seven missing geometric chords `ab` then has one further required equation:

\[
\boxed{
g_{ab}=\sum_{uv\in P_{a\to b}} g_{uv},}
\]

with signs chosen according to orientation, where `P_{a->b}` is the unique tree path from `a` to `b`.

Equivalently, add the missing chord `ab` to `T`. The resulting fundamental cycle must have zero cochain sum. In multiplicative sine form this is a finite product identity involving the missing-pair angle and the tree-path angles.

There are seven missing chords, and these seven equations are independent modulo the eight relation-cycle equations.

The exact verifier enumerates all 465 labelled 2-regular missing graphs,

\[
360\ C_7+105\ (C_3\sqcup C_4),
\]

constructs a deterministic spanning tree in each present graph, forms the eight present fundamental-cycle rows and seven missing-chord rows, and checks over `Q` that

\[
\operatorname{rank}(\text{present rows})=8,
\]

while

\[
\operatorname{rank}(\text{present rows}+\text{missing rows})=15.
\]

Thus the seven missing-pair identities add exactly seven independent metric constraints.

## 4. Why this is stronger than the existing relation-only metric layer

A realization cannot choose the geometry of a missing reciprocal pair independently. The two endpoints already have fixed radii and fixed ground-ray directions, so their geometric chord must agree with the same global radius potential as the fourteen support edges.

Therefore a nonlinear search that imposes only the eight relation cycles leaves seven metric degrees of inconsistency unchecked. The complete system can be imposed either as fifteen `K7` fundamental cycles or, more economically when extending existing code, as

\[
\boxed{
8\text{ present relation cycles}
+
7\text{ missing-chord cycles}.
}
\]

The latter form is useful for the current remaining branches because it reuses the relation-cycle machinery already present in the repository and adds exactly one equation per absent support edge.

For generic `H6`, this agrees with the complete-angular reconstruction theorem already proved in `target_c_support7_h6_complete_metric_integrability_2026-09-19.md`. The graph decomposition itself does not depend on the `H6` hull geometry and therefore also applies to generic `H7` and convex `H8` candidates.

The remaining work is not another graph-rank question. It is to intersect these fifteen metric equations with the branch-specific angular realization system and the seven exact equilateral-pentagon closures. Reciprocal-collinearity walls remain separate. C-II remains open.
